INT. NIGHT BUS - LOWER DECK - NIGHT

1953. London, fog thick as wet wool against the windows. The bus lurches through an empty Bayswater, its engine groaning like an old man climbing stairs.

Edith Marsh, 30s, a nurse in a navy coat with the cap folded in her lap, sits near the back. Her shoes are scuffed from a double shift. Across the aisle, Walter Penn, 60s, a conductor with a ticket machine strapped to his chest, leans on the rail and yawns without apology.

ANGLE ON the dim bulb overhead, flickering, throwing yellow across faces.

Near the front, Alistair Crane, 40s, in a good tweed overcoat too warm for the night, reads a folded newspaper he is not reading, while beside him a boy, Tommy, 10, in a cap too big for him, kicks his heels against the seat and watches the rain run in lines down the glass, and Walter checks his watch and the clock above the driver and the road ahead, all in one practised sweep.

                    WALTER
Fares, please. Fares. Last bus tonight, ladies and gentlemen. Last bus and last chance.

                    EDITH
(tired, smiling)
You say that every night, Mr Penn.

                    WALTER
And every night I mean it, Miss Marsh.

WE SEE Alistair's gloved hand tighten on the newspaper.

                    ALISTAIR
(without looking up)
The boy has no ticket.

                    TOMMY
(small)
Da says it's all right.

                    ALISTAIR
Does he.

                    WALTER
(gently)
Where's your da, lad?

                    TOMMY
Home. He's poorly.

Silence. The bus turns a corner, and everyone sways together.

CLOSE ON Tommy's coat sleeve. The cuff is worn through. Edith sees it.

                    EDITH
(rising, crossing the aisle)
I'll pay his fare. Here. A tuppence, is it?

                    WALTER
Threepence from here, miss.

                    EDITH
Then threepence.

She sets the coins in the machine's tray. Alistair folds the newspaper at last, slow and exact, and looks at her for the first time.

                    ALISTAIR
That's very generous. I wonder you can afford it, on a nurse's wage.

                    EDITH
(level)
I wonder you can afford that coat, on whatever it is you do.

                    WALTER
(a warning)
Now then.

                    ALISTAIR
I do very well, thank you. I take an interest in people who leave their patients at night.

                    EDITH
I haven't left anyone.

                    ALISTAIR
(softly)
Mrs Harcourt, in the end bed. She'd be a good deal more comfortable if you'd stayed.

Edith goes still. The engine drones. Tommy has stopped kicking.

BACK TO SCENE: the bus slows at a stop where no one waits, the doors hiss, and cold air comes in carrying the smell of coal smoke.

                    EDITH
(very quiet)
Who are you?

                    ALISTAIR
Someone who reads the ward reports.

                    WALTER
I think, sir, you'd best get off here.

                    ALISTAIR
I think I'll ride to the terminus, conductor. I've paid.

                    TOMMY
(tugging Edith's sleeve)
Miss, is he a policeman?

                    EDITH
(a long beat)
No, love. I think he's only lonely.

Alistair's face flickers, the tiniest crack. He looks at the boy, then at the fogged window, then at his own reflection, which looks like someone he would rather not be.

                    ALISTAIR
(low)
Mrs Harcourt died at nine. I was her son.

                    EDITH
(stricken)
I'm so sorry. I didn't know.

                    ALISTAIR
No. Nobody did. She said you were kind.

Walter takes off his cap. The doors hiss shut. The bus pulls away into the fog, and no one says anything else.
